WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.380
PII-related incidents require both security discipline and privacy discipline.

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:12.200
A security event is not always a security incident, and a security incident is not always a PII breach.

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:25.500
A privacy event may involve incorrect processing, unauthorized disclosure, retention beyond a defined period, misuse of support access, or a policy violation even when no external attacker is involved.

4
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.800
Contractual notification triggers and regulatory notification triggers can differ.

5
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.980
A reliable program defines these categories before an incident occurs.

6
00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:57.020
Operational response should cover detection, triage, containment, evidence preservation, scope analysis, data and tenant identification, customer identification, severity assessment, escalation, notification, investigation, eradication, recovery, lessons learned, and corrective action.

7
00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.760
In a multi-tenant cloud service, scope analysis is especially important.

8
00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:11.700
The provider should be able to identify which service, region, tenant, data category, administrative account, subprocessor, or support process was involved.

9
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.680
Without that evidence, notification and remediation become guesswork.

10
00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:35.200
Contracts and procedures should define notification contacts, notification triggers, initial notification expectations, update cadence, required information, secure communication channels, cooperation responsibilities, evidence preservation, root-cause reporting, and corrective-action tracking.

11
00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:44.260
This course does not invent a universal notification deadline because deadlines arise from applicable law, regulation, contract, and organizational policy.

12
00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:52.120
What matters operationally is that the provider and customer know which clock applies to which event and who is responsible for each communication.

13
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.800
Logging and monitoring support accountability, but logs are also data.

14
00:01:56.900 --> 00:02:13.620
Administrative actions, customer access, support access, data exports, configuration changes, authentication, deletion requests, privileged operations, subprocessor activity, and incident-response actions may all need logging.

15
00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:24.120
At the same time, logs can contain identifiers, IP addresses, object names, message fragments, search terms, support notes, or other PII.

16
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:31.580
They need access restrictions, retention rules, integrity protection, minimization, secure disposal, and monitoring.

17
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.660
The content of logs should be deliberate.

18
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.720
Too little logging makes investigation impossible.

19
00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:44.940
Too much logging can create unnecessary PII stores and expose sensitive details to administrators who do not need them.

20
00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:58.800
Good designs capture who did what, when, from where, through which approved path, against which tenant or data set, and with what outcome, while avoiding full payload capture unless a defined and controlled need exists.

21
00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.000
Accountability is shown through evidence.

22
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:18.220
A policy that says incidents are handled appropriately is weaker than records showing triage decisions, containment actions, customer communications, legal review, secure evidence handling, root-cause analysis, corrective action, and management review.

23
00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:23.440
The evidence should be reliable, protected, and retained according to policy.

24
00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.980
It should also be fresh enough to demonstrate current operation.

25
00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:31.100
After an incident or exercise, the program should improve.

26
00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:46.240
Corrective actions may include technical configuration changes, access review, support-process updates, contract language updates, subprocessor review, training, monitoring improvements, or changes to retention and deletion procedures.

27
00:03:46.340 --> 00:04:00.420
ISO/IEC 27018 supports accountability by encouraging disciplined PII protection in cloud processor contexts, but accountability becomes real only when incidents generate learning and traceable remediation.

28
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:05.180
Exercises are valuable because they reveal whether procedures are usable.

29
00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:20.400
A tabletop can test whether the provider can identify affected tenants, determine whether logs contain PII, notify the correct customer contact through a secure channel, preserve evidence, involve legal and privacy specialists, and track corrective actions.

30
00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:27.700
A technical simulation can test whether monitoring detects unusual data export or privileged support activity.

31
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:35.000
The exercise should produce findings, owners, due dates, and management review, not just a statement that the team met.

32
00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.240
Customers also benefit from this discipline.

33
00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:44.980
Clear incident commitments help them route internal privacy, legal, security, and business decisions quickly.

34
00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:52.040
In cloud PII processing, accountability often depends on both parties having usable information at the right time.

35
00:04:52.140 --> 00:05:01.740
The provider cannot make every customer decision, and the customer cannot investigate provider systems alone, so the response model has to be cooperative before pressure arrives.
